1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,884
Some prices matter more than others, because of what traders did there.

2
00:00:03,934 --> 00:00:06,303
The chart can show five kinds of them.

3
00:00:36,418 --> 00:00:38,894
We're on Bitcoin, with four-hour candles.

4
00:00:39,359 --> 00:00:43,798
Liquidation levels estimate where leveraged traders would be forced to close.

5
00:00:43,848 --> 00:00:48,713
Red for longs below the price, green for shorts above. Brighter means more money.

6
00:00:49,172 --> 00:00:53,013
Fair value gaps are price ranges a fast move jumped across.

7
00:00:53,063 --> 00:00:56,563
Green ones were left by rises, red ones by falls.

8
00:00:56,613 --> 00:00:59,708
Each stays shaded until price trades back through it.

9
00:01:02,424 --> 00:01:07,289
Single prints, in violet, are prices only one half hour of a day traded through.

10
00:01:07,339 --> 00:01:10,455
Thin areas, which price often comes back to fill.

11
00:01:14,556 --> 00:01:17,096
The anchor tool draws an anchored VWAP.

12
00:01:17,146 --> 00:01:21,393
Tap a candle, such as a swing low, to see the average price paid since then,

13
00:01:21,413 --> 00:01:22,779
weighted by volume.

14
00:01:23,673 --> 00:01:27,450
The volume profile tool: drag across a stretch of the chart.

15
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:31,091
It shows at which prices most trading happened, the busiest price,

16
00:01:31,111 --> 00:01:34,200
and the band holding seventy percent of the volume.

17
00:01:34,673 --> 00:01:36,701
None of these is a forecast.

18
00:01:36,751 --> 00:01:41,147
They show where price has reason to pause or turn, so you can plan around them.
